// New articles, November 2026 (second batch): five world cities, four ancient sites, the Titanic, museums, libraries, board games, the tundra, woolly mammoths, Neanderthals, the Suez Canal, concrete, Carthage and the Asante Empire.
addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{seoul:[126.98,37.57,"South Korea",36,"Seoul"],venice:[12.34,45.44,"Italy",36,"Venice"],moscow:[37.62,55.75,"Russia",36,"Moscow"],buenosaires:[-58.38,-34.60,"Argentina",36,"Buenos Aires"],dubai:[55.27,25.20,"United Arab Emirates",36,"Dubai"],stonehenge:[-1.83,51.18,"United Kingdom",36,"Stonehenge"],chichenitza:[-88.57,20.68,"Mexico",36,"Chichén Itzá"],easterisland:[-109.35,-27.12,"Chile",36,"Easter Island"],pompeii:[14.48,40.75,"Italy",36,"Pompeii"],carthage:[10.32,36.85,"Tunisia",36,"Carthage"],suezcanal:[32.35,30.60,"Egypt",36,"Suez Canal"]})});

ADDART({id:"seoul",t:"Seoul",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏙️",w:"Seoul",r:["southkoreacountry","korea","kpop","megacities","koreanwar"],
p:[["South Korea’s capital","Seoul is the capital and largest city of South Korea, on the Han River."],["Six centuries old","It has been Korea’s capital since 1394, with royal palaces from the Joseon dynasty."],["High-tech and creative","Today it is a center of technology, design and the Korean Wave of pop culture."]],
l:"Seoul is the capital and largest city of South Korea, with about 9.4 million people, while its wider metropolitan area holds around half of the country’s population of 52 million. It lies in the northwest of the country on the Han River. Seoul became the capital of Korea’s Joseon dynasty in 1394 and has remained the country’s capital ever since. Badly damaged in the Korean War, it was rebuilt and grew at astonishing speed into one of the world’s great high-tech cities. Seoul is home to royal palaces, Buddhist temples, traditional hanok houses, glass skyscrapers, major technology companies and the entertainment industry behind K-pop and Korean dramas.",
s:[["A royal capital","In 1394, the founder of the Joseon dynasty made the city, then called Hanyang, his capital and surrounded it with a wall and gates. Gyeongbokgung, the main royal palace, was built in 1395. Joseon kings ruled from Seoul until 1910, when Japan annexed Korea. The name Seoul simply means ‘capital’."],["War and rebuilding","During the Korean War of 1950–53, Seoul changed hands four times and much of it was destroyed. In the following decades, South Korea’s rapid industrialization, sometimes called the ‘Miracle on the Han River’, turned Seoul into a booming city of apartment towers, factories, offices and highways."],["Modern Seoul","Seoul hosted the 1988 Summer Olympics, which showed the world South Korea’s rise. It has one of the world’s largest subway systems, very fast internet and many technology companies. Neighborhoods such as Gangnam, Hongdae and Myeongdong are famous for shopping, music, fashion and food."],["Old and new together","Five grand palaces from the Joseon era survive, along with the Bukchon area of traditional hanok houses. In 2005, the city removed an elevated highway to restore the Cheonggyecheon stream, creating a popular green walkway through the city center."]],
k:[["Country","South Korea"],["Population","About 9.4 million (city)"],["River","Han"],["Capital since","1394"],["Olympics","1988"]],
d:[["1394","Becomes the capital of the Joseon dynasty"],["1395","Gyeongbokgung Palace is built"],["1910","Japan annexes Korea"],["1950–53","The Korean War devastates the city"],["1988","Hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2005","The Cheonggyecheon stream is restored"]],
s2:[["The Cheonggyecheon","For decades, the Cheonggyecheon stream was covered by a road and an elevated highway. In 2003–2005, the city tore down the highway and restored a 10.9-kilometer stream with walkways, bridges and plants. It cooled the surrounding area, brought back wildlife and became one of the city’s favorite places to stroll."],["The changing of the guard","At Gyeongbokgung Palace, a re-enactment of the Joseon-era changing of the royal guard takes place several times a day, with guards in colorful traditional costume carrying flags and weapons to the sound of drums. Visitors who wear hanbok, traditional Korean clothing, can enter the palace for free."]],
sp:["Namsan Tower","N Seoul Tower stands on Namsan, a mountain in the middle of the city, offering views across Seoul. Couples attach ‘love locks’ to the railings around it, and the tower is lit up at night with colors that can show the level of air pollution."],
g:[["Hanok","A traditional Korean house"],["Hanbok","Traditional Korean clothing"],["Joseon","The Korean dynasty that ruled from 1392 to 1910"],["Hallyu","The Korean Wave of popular culture"]],
f:["The name Seoul means ‘capital’.","About half of South Korea’s people live in the Seoul metropolitan area.","Seoul changed hands four times during the Korean War."],
fun:"Seoul’s subway trains have heated seats in winter.",
tl:"Ang Seoul ang kabisera at pinakamalaking lungsod ng South Korea, sa tabi ng Ilog Han. Kabisera na ito mula pa noong 1394, at ngayon ay sentro ng teknolohiya at ng Korean Wave."});

ADDART({id:"venice",t:"Venice",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛶",w:"Venice",r:["italycountry","renais","ships","silk","tourism"],
p:[["A city on water","Venice is built on more than a hundred small islands in a lagoon in northeast Italy."],["A sea power","For a thousand years, the Republic of Venice was a great trading and naval power."],["Under threat","Rising seas, flooding and mass tourism challenge the city today."]],
l:"Venice is a city in northeastern Italy built on 118 small islands in a shallow lagoon off the Adriatic Sea, linked by about 400 bridges and crossed by some 150 canals instead of streets. People settled in the lagoon from the 5th century to escape invaders, and Venice grew into a wealthy independent republic that dominated trade between Europe and the East for centuries. Its palaces, churches and art, including St Mark’s Basilica, the Doge’s Palace and the works of painters such as Titian, make it one of the most beautiful cities in the world. Today Venice faces serious challenges from flooding, sinking land, a shrinking resident population and very large numbers of tourists.",
s:[["Building on water","The first Venetians drove millions of wooden piles into the mud of the lagoon and built stone foundations on top. Because the wood stayed underwater without oxygen, it did not rot, and many buildings still stand on piles hundreds of years old. The Grand Canal, about 3.8 kilometers long, winds through the city in an S shape."],["The Most Serene Republic","For about 1,100 years, Venice was an independent republic led by an elected ruler called the doge. Its merchants traded spices, silk and other goods from Asia, and its shipyard, the Arsenal, could build a warship in a single day. Venetian traveler Marco Polo journeyed to China. The republic ended in 1797, when Napoleon’s army took the city."],["Art and culture","Venice was a great center of Renaissance art, music and printing. Painters such as Titian, Tintoretto and Canaletto worked there, and the composer Antonio Vivaldi was born there. The Venice Biennale art exhibition began in 1895, and the Venice Film Festival, founded in 1932, is the oldest film festival in the world."],["Floods and tourists","Venice regularly floods during high tides called acqua alta. Since 2020, a system of mobile barriers called MOSE has been raised at the lagoon’s inlets to hold back the highest tides. The historic center now has fewer than 50,000 residents but receives millions of visitors a year, and since 2024 day-trippers have paid an entry fee on busy days."]],
k:[["Country","Italy"],["Islands","118"],["Bridges","About 400"],["Main canal","The Grand Canal"],["UNESCO site","Since 1987"]],
d:[["400s","People settle in the lagoon to escape invaders"],["697","Traditional date of the first doge"],["1271","Marco Polo sets out for Asia"],["1797","Napoleon ends the Venetian Republic"],["1866","Venice becomes part of Italy"],["2020","The MOSE flood barriers are first raised"]],
s2:[["Gondolas","Gondolas are long, flat-bottomed boats steered by a gondolier standing at the back with a single oar. They were once the main way to get around Venice; today they are mostly used by tourists, while residents use water buses called vaporetti. Gondolas are built slightly lopsided so they travel straight with a single oar."],["The Carnival","The Carnival of Venice, held in the weeks before Lent, is famous for its elaborate masks and costumes. Masks allowed people of all classes to mix freely. The tradition faded after the fall of the republic and was revived in 1979."]],
sp:["Acqua alta","When strong winds and high tides combine, water rises over the lowest parts of the city, especially St Mark’s Square. In November 2019, the water reached 1.87 meters above normal, the second-highest on record, flooding much of the city and damaging St Mark’s Basilica."],
g:[["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"],["Doge","The elected ruler of the Venetian Republic"],["Gondola","A traditional flat-bottomed Venetian rowing boat"],["Acqua alta","An exceptionally high tide that floods Venice"]],
f:["Venice has about 400 bridges.","The Venice Film Festival is the oldest film festival in the world.","Venetian buildings stand on wooden piles driven into the mud."],
fun:"There are no cars in the historic center of Venice; even ambulances and fire engines are boats.",
tl:"Ang Venice ay lungsod sa hilagang-silangan ng Italya na itinayo sa mahigit isandaang maliliit na pulo sa isang lagoon. Naging makapangyarihang republika ito sa kalakalan, at ngayon ay hinaharap nito ang pagbaha at napakaraming turista."});

ADDART({id:"moscow",t:"Moscow",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Moscow",r:["russiacountry","russrev","coldwar","ussrfall","napoleon"],
p:[["Russia’s capital","Moscow is the capital and largest city of Russia."],["The Kremlin","At its heart is the Kremlin, a walled fortress that is the seat of government."],["A long history","It grew from a small medieval town into the center of a vast state."]],
l:"Moscow is the capital and largest city of Russia, with about 13 million people, making it one of the largest cities in Europe. It stands on the Moskva River in the west of the country. First mentioned in 1147, Moscow grew from a small fortified town into the capital of the Grand Principality of Moscow, which gathered the Russian lands into one state. Except for two centuries when St Petersburg was the capital, it has been the center of Russian power, under the tsars, the Soviet Union and modern Russia. Its landmarks include the Kremlin, Red Square, St Basil’s Cathedral, the Bolshoi Theater and the ornate stations of the Moscow Metro.",
s:[["Beginnings","Moscow first appears in the chronicles in 1147, when Prince Yuri Dolgoruky held a feast there. It was burned by the Mongols in 1238 but grew in power under the Mongol-Tatar rulers. In the 15th and 16th centuries, under Ivan III and Ivan IV, known as Ivan the Terrible, Moscow became the capital of a powerful, expanding Russian state."],["The Kremlin and Red Square","The Kremlin is a fortified complex of palaces, cathedrals and government buildings surrounded by red brick walls with 20 towers, built mostly in the late 15th century. Beside it lies Red Square, whose name comes from an old Russian word that meant both ‘red’ and ‘beautiful’. The colorful onion domes of St Basil’s Cathedral, built in 1555–1561, stand at one end."],["Fire and war","In 1712, Peter the Great moved the capital to his new city of St Petersburg. When Napoleon captured Moscow in 1812, much of the city burned, and his army was forced into a disastrous retreat. In 1918, the Bolshevik government moved the capital back to Moscow. In late 1941, Soviet forces stopped the German army at the gates of the city."],["Soviet and modern Moscow","Under Soviet rule, Moscow gained wide avenues, huge apartment blocks and seven towering ‘Stalinist’ skyscrapers. The Metro, opened in 1935, is famous for stations decorated like palaces, with chandeliers, mosaics and statues. Since the 1990s, the city has added a skyline of modern towers in its business district."]],
k:[["Country","Russia"],["Population","About 13 million"],["River","Moskva"],["First mentioned","1147"],["Seat of government","The Kremlin"]],
d:[["1147","First mention of Moscow"],["1238","Burned by the Mongols"],["1561","St Basil’s Cathedral is completed"],["1712","The capital moves to St Petersburg"],["1812","Napoleon occupies Moscow; a great fire"],["1918","Moscow becomes the capital again"],["1935","The Moscow Metro opens"]],
s2:[["The Moscow Metro","When the first Metro line opened in 1935, Soviet leaders wanted its stations to be ‘palaces for the people’. Stations such as Komsomolskaya and Mayakovskaya are decorated with marble, mosaics, chandeliers and sculptures. Today the system carries millions of passengers a day and has over 250 stations."],["The Bolshoi Theater","The Bolshoi Theater, whose name means ‘big’, is one of the most famous homes of ballet and opera in the world. Its company dates from 1776, and its grand theater has been rebuilt several times after fires. Swan Lake by Tchaikovsky premiered there in 1877."]],
sp:["St Basil’s Cathedral","St Basil’s Cathedral was built on Red Square by order of Ivan the Terrible to celebrate military victories. It is made up of nine chapels, each with its own brightly painted onion dome. According to legend, Ivan had the architects blinded so they could never build anything so beautiful again, but historians consider this a myth."],
g:[["Kremlin","A fortress in a Russian city, especially the one in Moscow"],["Tsar","The title of the emperors of Russia"],["Onion dome","A rounded dome that comes to a point, like an onion"],["Metro","An underground railway system"]],
f:["Red Square’s name comes from a word that once meant both ‘red’ and ‘beautiful’.","The Kremlin walls have 20 towers.","Moscow’s Metro opened in 1935."],
fun:"The Kremlin’s main towers are topped with red stars that are lit from inside and slowly turn with the wind.",
tl:"Ang Moscow ang kabisera at pinakamalaking lungsod ng Russia. Nasa puso nito ang Kremlin, isang napapaderang kuta na upuan ng pamahalaan, at lumaki ito mula sa maliit na bayan hanggang maging sentro ng isang malawak na bansa."});

ADDART({id:"buenosaires",t:"Buenos Aires",c:"Countries and Places",i:"💃",w:"Buenos Aires",r:["argentinacountry","latinind","soccer","megacities","opera"],
p:[["Argentina’s capital","Buenos Aires is the capital and largest city of Argentina, on the Río de la Plata."],["Birthplace of tango","The tango dance and music were born in its port neighborhoods."],["Paris of South America","Its grand avenues and theaters earned it this nickname."]],
l:"Buenos Aires is the capital and largest city of Argentina, located on the western shore of the Río de la Plata estuary. The city itself has about 3 million people, and its metropolitan area over 15 million, making it one of the largest urban areas in South America. Founded by the Spanish in the 16th century, it grew into a major port and, with waves of immigrants from Italy, Spain and elsewhere in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, a cosmopolitan city of wide boulevards, grand buildings and theaters. It is famous as the birthplace of tango, for its passion for football, its cafés and bookshops, and its steak and late-night dining. Its residents are called porteños, ‘people of the port’.",
s:[["Founding","Spanish explorer Pedro de Mendoza founded a settlement there in 1536, but it was abandoned after attacks and hunger. Juan de Garay refounded it in 1580. For a long time, Spain restricted its trade, but it grew as a port, and in 1776 it became the capital of a new Spanish viceroyalty."],["Independence","On 25 May 1810, in the May Revolution, residents of Buenos Aires replaced the Spanish viceroy with a local government, a key step towards Argentina’s independence in 1816. The date is a national holiday, and the city’s central Plaza de Mayo, in front of the presidential palace, the Casa Rosada, is named after it."],["A city of immigrants","Between 1880 and 1930, millions of European immigrants arrived, many from Italy and Spain. They shaped the city’s food, language and culture: Buenos Aires Spanish has a strong Italian-influenced accent and its own slang, called lunfardo. In 1913, the city opened the first underground railway in Latin America."],["Culture and sport","The Teatro Colón, opened in 1908, is one of the world’s finest opera houses, famous for its acoustics. The city has more bookshops per person than almost any other. Football rivalries run deep, especially the ‘Superclásico’ between Boca Juniors and River Plate."]],
k:[["Country","Argentina"],["Population","About 3 million (city); over 15 million (metro)"],["Waterway","Río de la Plata"],["Residents called","Porteños"],["Famous dance","Tango"]],
d:[["1536","First Spanish settlement founded"],["1580","Refounded by Juan de Garay"],["1810","The May Revolution"],["1880","Becomes Argentina’s federal capital"],["1908","Teatro Colón opens"],["1913","First underground railway in Latin America"]],
s2:[["The birth of tango","Tango developed in the late 19th century in the poor port neighborhoods of Buenos Aires and Montevideo, mixing African, European and local influences. At first considered scandalous, it became a craze in Paris and around the world in the early 1900s. In 2009, UNESCO recognized tango as part of humanity’s intangible cultural heritage."],["Avenida 9 de Julio","Avenida 9 de Julio, named after Argentina’s independence day, is one of the widest avenues in the world, with up to seven lanes in each direction plus side roads. At its center stands the Obelisco, a white obelisk built in 1936 to mark the 400th anniversary of the city’s first founding."]],
sp:["La Boca","La Boca, an old port neighborhood settled by Italian immigrants, is famous for its brightly painted houses made of corrugated metal, said to have been painted with leftover paint from the shipyards. Its street Caminito is lined with tango dancers and artists, and it is home to the Boca Juniors football club."],
g:[["Porteño","A person from Buenos Aires"],["Tango","A dance and music that began in Buenos Aires and Montevideo"],["Estuary","The wide mouth of a river where it meets the sea"],["Lunfardo","Slang from Buenos Aires"]],
f:["Buenos Aires means ‘fair winds’ or ‘good airs’ in Spanish.","Its underground railway opened in 1913.","Pope Francis was born in Buenos Aires in 1936."],
fun:"Many porteños eat dinner at 10 pm or later.",
tl:"Ang Buenos Aires ang kabisera at pinakamalaking lungsod ng Argentina, sa pampang ng Río de la Plata. Dito isinilang ang tango, at tinatawag itong ‘Paris ng Timog Amerika’ dahil sa malalapad na kalye at teatro."});

ADDART({id:"dubai",t:"Dubai",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏗️",w:"Dubai",r:["uaecountry","skyscrapers","desert","tourism","trade"],
p:[["A desert boomtown","Dubai grew from a small trading port into a global city in a few decades."],["Record-breaking towers","It is home to the Burj Khalifa, the tallest building in the world."],["A city of newcomers","Most of its residents were born in other countries."]],
l:"Dubai is the most populous city of the United Arab Emirates and the capital of the emirate of the same name, one of the seven that make up the country. It lies on the southeastern coast of the Persian Gulf, at the edge of the Arabian Desert, and has about 3.8 million residents, the large majority of them foreign workers and expatriates. A small fishing, pearling and trading town for most of its history, Dubai changed rapidly after oil was discovered in 1966, investing its wealth in ports, airlines, finance, tourism and real estate rather than relying on oil. Today it is known for its skyscrapers, including the Burj Khalifa, artificial islands, huge shopping malls and one of the world’s busiest airports.",
s:[["Pearls and trade","For centuries, the people of Dubai lived by fishing, pearl diving and trade along Dubai Creek, a natural inlet that served as a harbor. Its traditional souks, or markets, sold gold, spices and textiles. The pearl industry collapsed in the 1930s, when cultured pearls from Japan became available and the Great Depression struck."],["Oil and a new nation","Oil was discovered offshore in 1966. In 1971, Dubai joined Abu Dhabi and other emirates to form the United Arab Emirates. Its rulers used oil money to build a deep-water port, a free trade zone and an airline. Because Dubai’s oil reserves were relatively small, it focused on becoming a hub for business, travel and tourism."],["Building big","From the 1990s, Dubai launched dramatic building projects: the sail-shaped Burj Al Arab hotel, the palm-shaped artificial island Palm Jumeirah, and in 2010 the 828-meter Burj Khalifa. The Dubai Metro, opened in 2009, runs driverless trains. The city hosted the world’s fair, Expo 2020, in 2021–2022."],["Life in Dubai","People from more than 200 nationalities live in Dubai, with large communities from South and Southeast Asia, Europe and other Arab countries. Summer temperatures often exceed 40°C, so life moves indoors into air-conditioned malls. Drinking water comes mostly from desalination plants. Rights groups have raised concerns about conditions for some migrant workers."]],
k:[["Country","United Arab Emirates"],["Population","About 3.8 million"],["Tallest building","Burj Khalifa, 828 m"],["Oil discovered","1966"],["Joined the UAE","1971"]],
d:[["1833","The Al Maktoum family settles at Dubai Creek"],["1930s","The pearl trade collapses"],["1966","Oil is discovered offshore"],["1971","Dubai helps found the United Arab Emirates"],["2009","The Dubai Metro opens"],["2010","The Burj Khalifa opens"]],
s2:[["The Burj Khalifa","The Burj Khalifa, opened in January 2010, is 828 meters tall, the tallest building in the world. Its design was partly inspired by the shape of a desert flower. It has over 160 floors, and on a clear day its tip can be seen from about 95 kilometers away."],["Islands made of sand","Palm Jumeirah was built by dredging sand from the seabed and spraying it into a palm-tree shape, protected by a crescent-shaped breakwater of rock. It holds hotels, homes and beaches. Other planned islands, including ‘The World’, shaped like a map of the continents, have been slower to develop."]],
sp:["Dubai Creek and the abra","Dubai Creek divides the old city into two parts, Deira and Bur Dubai. Small wooden boats called abras have ferried people across it for generations and still carry passengers for a small fare, past traditional sailing ships called dhows loaded with goods."],
g:[["Emirate","A territory ruled by an emir; the UAE has seven"],["Souk","A traditional Arab market"],["Expatriate","A person living outside their home country"],["Desalination","Removing salt from seawater"]],
f:["The Burj Khalifa is 828 meters tall.","Most Dubai residents were born outside the UAE.","Dubai’s metro trains have no drivers."],
fun:"Because the Burj Khalifa is so tall, people at the top can watch the sun set a few minutes after people at the bottom.",
tl:"Ang Dubai ay lungsod sa United Arab Emirates na mabilis na lumaki mula sa maliit na daungan tungo sa pandaigdigang lungsod. Narito ang Burj Khalifa, ang pinakamataas na gusali sa mundo, at karamihan sa mga residente nito ay mula sa ibang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"stonehenge",t:"Stonehenge",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🪨",w:"Stonehenge",r:["stoneage","archaeology","sun","ukcountry","caveart"],
p:[["A prehistoric monument","Stonehenge is a ring of huge standing stones in southern England, built about 5,000 years ago."],["Stones from afar","Some stones were brought from Wales, over 200 kilometers away."],["Lined up with the sun","It is aligned with sunrise at the summer solstice and sunset at the winter solstice."]],
l:"Stonehenge is a prehistoric monument on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, England, made of a ring of massive standing stones topped by horizontal lintels, with smaller stones inside. It was built in several stages between about 3000 and 1500 BC by people of the Neolithic and Bronze Age, without metal tools, wheels or draft animals as far as we know. The large sarsen stones, weighing around 25 tonnes each, came from about 25 kilometers away, while the smaller bluestones were brought from the Preseli Hills in Wales, over 200 kilometers away. Stonehenge is aligned with the sunrise on the summer solstice and the sunset on the winter solstice. Its exact purpose remains debated.",
s:[["Building in stages","The first Stonehenge, around 3000 BC, was a circular ditch and bank, used as a burial site. Around 2500 BC, builders raised the great sarsen stones in a circle, with five huge trilithons, pairs of uprights topped by a lintel, in a horseshoe inside. The bluestones were rearranged several times over the following centuries."],["Moving the stones","How the builders moved stones weighing many tonnes is still studied. They probably used wooden sledges pulled on log rails or tracks by large teams of people, and perhaps rafts for part of the bluestones’ journey. The stones were shaped with stone hammers, and the lintels fit onto the uprights using joints copied from woodworking."],["What was it for?","Stonehenge may have been a temple, a place to honor ancestors, a ceremonial site linked to the seasons, or all of these. Hundreds of people were buried there, and nearby Durrington Walls was a large settlement, perhaps where builders and visitors feasted. Its solar alignment suggests that midsummer and midwinter were important times."],["Stonehenge today","Stonehenge and the surrounding landscape, rich in burial mounds and other monuments, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986. Thousands of people gather there to watch the sunrise at the summer solstice. Visitors usually view the stones from a path around them to protect the site."]],
k:[["Location","Wiltshire, England"],["Built","About 3000–1500 BC"],["Largest stones","Sarsens, about 25 tonnes"],["Bluestones from","Preseli Hills, Wales"],["UNESCO site","Since 1986"]],
d:[["c.3000 BC","A circular ditch and bank are dug"],["c.2500 BC","The great sarsen circle and trilithons are raised"],["c.2300 BC","Bluestones are rearranged"],["c.1500 BC","Final changes are made"],["1986","Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2024","Study traces the Altar Stone to northern Scotland"]],
s2:[["The Altar Stone","The large Altar Stone at the center of Stonehenge was long thought to come from Wales. In 2024, scientists analyzing tiny mineral grains in the stone found that it came from northeast Scotland, more than 700 kilometers away, showing how far the builders’ networks reached."],["Solstice sunrise","At the summer solstice, the sun rises almost directly over the Heel Stone, a large stone outside the circle, when seen from the center. At the winter solstice, the sun sets in line with the main axis on the opposite side. Many archaeologists think midwinter gatherings may have been the most important."]],
sp:["Durrington Walls","About 3 kilometers from Stonehenge lies Durrington Walls, a huge henge enclosure where archaeologists found the remains of houses and enormous amounts of pig bones from feasts held around midwinter. It may have been where the builders of Stonehenge lived and celebrated."],
g:[["Neolithic","The New Stone Age, when farming began"],["Sarsen","A type of hard sandstone used for Stonehenge’s large stones"],["Trilithon","Two upright stones with a third laid across the top"],["Solstice","The longest or shortest day of the year"]],
f:["The biggest sarsen stones weigh around 25 tonnes.","Some bluestones traveled over 200 kilometers from Wales.","Stonehenge’s builders used woodworking joints to fix stones together."],
fun:"Stonehenge is older than the Great Pyramid of Giza; its first stage was built several centuries earlier.",
tl:"Ang Stonehenge ay sinaunang bilog ng malalaking nakatayong bato sa timog Inglatera, itinayo mga 5,000 taon na ang nakararaan. Ang ilang bato ay dinala mula sa Wales, at nakahanay ito sa pagsikat ng araw tuwing summer solstice."});

ADDART({id:"chichenitza",t:"Chichén Itzá",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Chichen Itza",r:["maya","aztec","mexicocountry","archaeology","constellations"],
p:[["A great Maya city","Chichén Itzá was one of the largest cities of the Maya, in Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula."],["The pyramid of Kukulcán","Its famous step pyramid has 365 steps, one for each day of the year."],["A shadow serpent","At the equinoxes, a shadow like a snake appears on its stairs."]],
l:"Chichén Itzá is a large ancient city built by the Maya on the Yucatán Peninsula in what is now Mexico. It flourished from around AD 600 to 1200 and became one of the most powerful cities in the Maya world, a center of politics, trade and religion. Its buildings include El Castillo, also called the Temple of Kukulcán, a step pyramid about 30 meters tall; the Great Ball Court, the largest in ancient Mesoamerica; the Temple of the Warriors; an observatory known as El Caracol; and the Sacred Cenote, a natural sinkhole where offerings were thrown. Chichén Itzá is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and was chosen as one of the New Seven Wonders of the World in 2007.",
s:[["Rise and power","Chichén Itzá grew in a dry limestone region without rivers, where cenotes, natural sinkholes, provided water. Its name means ‘at the mouth of the well of the Itzá’. From around AD 900, it became the leading power in northern Yucatán, trading salt, cotton, jade, obsidian and other goods across Mesoamerica. Its art shows influences from central Mexico, which historians still debate."],["El Castillo","The Temple of Kukulcán, a feathered serpent god, has four staircases of 91 steps each; with the platform at the top, they total 365, the number of days in the solar year. Inside it, archaeologists found an older, smaller pyramid containing a red jaguar throne. Its design reflects the Maya’s detailed knowledge of astronomy and calendars."],["The ball game","The Great Ball Court is about 168 meters long, with stone rings set high on its walls. Players tried to keep a heavy rubber ball in the air using their hips, and to pass it through the rings. The game had religious meaning, and carvings on the court show a player being sacrificed."],["Decline and fame","Chichén Itzá declined after about 1200, though pilgrims continued to visit its Sacred Cenote. Spanish conquerors arrived in the 16th century. Today it is one of Mexico’s most visited archaeological sites, especially at the spring and autumn equinoxes."]],
k:[["Country","Mexico"],["Built by","The Maya"],["Height","About 600–1200"],["Famous pyramid","Temple of Kukulcán (El Castillo)"],["UNESCO site","Since 1988"]],
d:[["c.600","Chichén Itzá begins to grow"],["c.900–1100","At the height of its power"],["c.1200","The city declines"],["1500s","Spanish conquerors reach Yucatán"],["1988","Listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2007","Named one of the New Seven Wonders"]],
s2:[["The serpent of light","At the spring and autumn equinoxes, the late-afternoon sun casts a series of triangular shadows along the edge of El Castillo’s northern staircase, looking like a giant snake crawling down to join the carved serpent head at the bottom. Thousands of visitors gather to watch."],["The Sacred Cenote","The Sacred Cenote, a natural well about 60 meters across, was a place of pilgrimage. Divers have recovered gold, jade, pottery, incense and human remains from its waters, showing that offerings, and sometimes people, were sacrificed to the rain god Chaac."]],
sp:["A clapping echo","If you clap your hands in front of the stairs of El Castillo, the echo sounds like a chirp. Scientists have suggested it resembles the call of the quetzal, a bird sacred to the Maya, though no one knows if the effect was intended."],
g:[["Cenote","A natural sinkhole filled with groundwater"],["Kukulcán","The Maya feathered serpent god"],["Equinox","A day when day and night are about equal in length"],["Mesoamerica","The region of ancient civilizations in Mexico and Central America"]],
f:["El Castillo has 365 steps in total.","The Great Ball Court is about 168 meters long.","A smaller pyramid is hidden inside El Castillo."],
fun:"Clapping in front of El Castillo makes an echo that sounds like a bird’s chirp.",
tl:"Ang Chichén Itzá ay isa sa pinakamalalaking lungsod ng mga Maya sa Yucatán, Mexico. Ang tanyag na piramide nito ay may 365 baitang, at tuwing equinox ay may aninong parang ahas na lumilitaw sa hagdan nito."});

ADDART({id:"easterisland",t:"Easter Island (Rapa Nui)",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗿",w:"Easter Island",r:["polynesia","chilecountry","deforestation","archaeology","pacific"],
p:[["A remote island","Easter Island lies in the Pacific Ocean, about 3,500 kilometers off the coast of Chile."],["Giant statues","Its people carved nearly a thousand huge stone figures called moai."],["Polynesian voyagers","It was settled by Polynesian navigators who crossed the Pacific in canoes."]],
l:"Easter Island, known to its people as Rapa Nui, is a small volcanic island in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, about 3,500 kilometers west of Chile, of which it is a special territory. It is one of the most remote inhabited islands on Earth. Polynesian voyagers settled it around AD 1200, sailing great distances in double-hulled canoes. Over the following centuries, the Rapa Nui people carved nearly 1,000 giant stone statues, called moai, representing their ancestors, most of them from the soft volcanic rock of the Rano Raraku quarry. The island’s name comes from the Dutch explorer who arrived on Easter Sunday in 1722. Rapa Nui National Park is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["Settling the island","Rapa Nui was one of the last places in Polynesia to be settled. Its settlers brought crops such as sweet potato and taro, chickens and Polynesian rats. The island was then covered in palm forest. Its people developed a rich culture, with chiefs, priests, carvers and a script called rongorongo that has never been deciphered."],["The moai","The moai are carved human figures with large heads, averaging about 4 meters tall, though some are much larger. Most were placed on stone platforms called ahu along the coast, facing inland to watch over the villages. Some wore cylinders of red stone, called pukao, on their heads. Hundreds of unfinished moai remain in the quarry."],["Moving the statues","Rapa Nui oral tradition says the moai ‘walked’ to their places. Experiments have shown that teams of people using ropes could rock an upright statue from side to side so that it ‘walked’ forward, which matches the legends and the shape of the statues and roads."],["Collapse and survival","When Europeans arrived, the forests were gone and the population was smaller than it had been. Scholars debate how much was caused by deforestation, by rats eating palm seeds, or by later events. Disease and slave raids from Peru in the 1860s devastated the population, which fell to about 111 people by 1877. The Rapa Nui culture survived and is being revived today."]],
k:[["Country","Chile"],["Distance from Chile","About 3,500 km"],["Settled","Around AD 1200"],["Statues","Nearly 1,000 moai"],["UNESCO site","Since 1995"]],
d:[["c.1200","Polynesian voyagers settle the island"],["c.1250–1500","Most moai are carved"],["1722","Dutch explorer Jacob Roggeveen arrives on Easter Sunday"],["1862–63","Slave raids from Peru"],["1888","Chile annexes the island"],["1995","Rapa Nui National Park becomes a UNESCO site"]],
s2:[["The birdman contest","In later centuries, the Rapa Nui held a yearly contest at the clifftop village of Orongo. Competitors climbed down cliffs and swam to a small islet to find the first egg of the sooty tern. The winner’s sponsor became the ‘birdman’, an important figure for the coming year. Rock carvings of birdmen can still be seen there."],["A statue far from home","One moai, called Hoa Hakananai’a, was taken by a British ship in 1868 and is now in the British Museum in London. The Rapa Nui people have asked for it to be returned, part of a wider debate about objects taken from their places of origin."]],
sp:["Rano Raraku","Rano Raraku is the volcanic crater where most moai were carved. Nearly 400 statues remain there, many buried up to their shoulders in soil, so that only their heads show. The largest unfinished moai, still attached to the rock, is about 21 meters long."],
g:[["Moai","A giant stone statue of Easter Island"],["Ahu","A stone platform on which moai stood"],["Rongorongo","An undeciphered script of Easter Island"],["Polynesia","A region of the Pacific with over 1,000 islands"]],
f:["The island’s Polynesian name is Rapa Nui.","Nearly 400 moai remain at the quarry where they were carved.","The rongorongo script has never been deciphered."],
fun:"Many moai have full bodies buried underground; only their heads and shoulders are visible.",
tl:"Ang Easter Island o Rapa Nui ay malayong pulo sa Pasipiko na sakop ng Chile. Doon gumawa ang mga tao ng halos isang libong malalaking estatwang bato na tinatawag na moai, at ito ay tinirhan ng mga Polynesian na naglayag sa karagatan."});

ADDART({id:"pompeii",t:"Pompeii",c:"History",i:"🌋",w:"Pompeii",r:["rome","volc","archaeology","italycountry","greece"],
p:[["Buried by a volcano","Pompeii was a Roman town buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in AD 79."],["Frozen in time","Its houses, streets, paintings and even food survived under the ash."],["A window on Roman life","Excavations since 1748 have revealed how ordinary Romans lived."]],
l:"Pompeii was a prosperous Roman town near the Bay of Naples in southern Italy, home to perhaps 11,000 people. In AD 79, the nearby volcano Mount Vesuvius erupted violently, burying Pompeii and neighboring towns such as Herculaneum under meters of ash and pumice. Many residents escaped, but over a thousand people’s remains have been found in Pompeii. The ash preserved the town in remarkable detail: streets, shops, temples, theaters, houses with colorful wall paintings, graffiti, and even loaves of bread. Rediscovered in the 18th century, Pompeii has been excavated for over 270 years and gives an unmatched picture of daily life in the Roman Empire. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["The eruption","The eruption began around midday with a huge column of ash and pumice rising tens of kilometers into the sky, which then rained down on Pompeii for hours, collapsing roofs. The next morning, scorching avalanches of hot gas and ash called pyroclastic flows swept through, killing those who remained. The traditional date is 24 August, but evidence such as autumn fruits suggests it may have been in October."],["An eyewitness","The Roman writer Pliny the Younger watched the eruption from across the bay and later described it in two letters, the first known eyewitness account of a volcanic eruption. His uncle, Pliny the Elder, an admiral and naturalist, died while trying to rescue people by ship. Eruptions of this kind are now called Plinian eruptions."],["Rediscovery","Pompeii was forgotten for centuries. Systematic excavations began in 1748. In the 1860s, archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli poured plaster into hollow spaces left in the ash by decayed bodies, creating casts that show the victims’ final positions. About two-thirds of the town has been excavated."],["Daily life revealed","Pompeii’s ruins include bakeries with ovens, snack bars with counters for hot food, public baths, an amphitheater, brothels, workshops and grand houses with gardens. Thousands of graffiti on walls record election slogans, jokes, love messages and advertisements. New discoveries are still made every year."]],
k:[["Country","Italy"],["Destroyed","AD 79"],["Volcano","Mount Vesuvius"],["Excavations began","1748"],["UNESCO site","Since 1997"]],
d:[["c.600s BC","A settlement grows at Pompeii"],["80 BC","Becomes a Roman colony"],["AD 62","Damaged by a strong earthquake"],["AD 79","Buried by the eruption of Vesuvius"],["1748","Excavations begin"],["1863","Fiorelli makes the first plaster casts"]],
s2:[["Herculaneum","The nearby town of Herculaneum was buried even deeper, by pyroclastic flows that hardened into rock up to 20 meters thick. This preserved wooden furniture, food and a library of carbonized papyrus scrolls. In the 2020s, scientists began reading words in these scrolls using X-ray scans and artificial intelligence."],["Cave canem","At the entrance of one Pompeii house is a mosaic of a fierce chained dog with the words ‘Cave canem’, ‘Beware of the dog’. It is one of the most famous images from Pompeii and shows that Romans, like people today, warned visitors about their guard dogs."]],
sp:["Vesuvius today","Mount Vesuvius last erupted in 1944 and is considered one of the most dangerous volcanoes in the world, because about 3 million people live nearby. Scientists monitor it constantly, and authorities have evacuation plans for the surrounding towns."],
g:[["Pumice","A light, porous volcanic rock full of air bubbles"],["Pyroclastic flow","A fast avalanche of hot gas, ash and rock"],["Excavation","Digging carefully to uncover remains"],["Fresco","A painting made on wet plaster"]],
f:["Pliny the Younger wrote the first eyewitness account of a volcanic eruption.","About two-thirds of Pompeii has been excavated.","Bakers’ loaves of bread were found preserved in Pompeii’s ovens."],
fun:"Graffiti found in Pompeii includes election posters, jokes and a message saying, in effect, ‘I was here’.",
tl:"Ang Pompeii ay bayang Romano na natabunan ng pagputok ng Bundok Vesuvius noong AD 79. Napreserba ng abo ang mga bahay, kalye, larawan at maging pagkain nito, kaya nakikita natin kung paano namuhay ang mga Romano."});

ADDART({id:"titanic",t:"The Titanic",c:"History",i:"🚢",w:"Titanic",r:["ships","glaciers","explor","radio","ukcountry"],
p:[["An ‘unsinkable’ ship","The Titanic was the largest ship in the world when it sailed in 1912."],["A night of disaster","It hit an iceberg on its first voyage and sank, killing more than 1,500 people."],["Lessons learned","The disaster led to new rules for safety at sea."]],
l:"The RMS Titanic was a British passenger liner that sank in the North Atlantic Ocean on 15 April 1912, during its maiden voyage from Southampton to New York, after striking an iceberg. Built in Belfast for the White Star Line, it was the largest moving object ever made at the time, about 269 meters long, and was famed for its luxury. Of the roughly 2,224 passengers and crew on board, more than 1,500 died, one of the deadliest peacetime sinkings in history. There were lifeboats for only about half of those aboard. The disaster shocked the world and led to major improvements in maritime safety. The wreck was found in 1985 at a depth of about 3,800 meters.",
s:[["The ship","The Titanic was one of three huge Olympic-class liners built by Harland and Wolff in Belfast. It had first-class suites, a grand staircase, a swimming pool and a gym, while third-class passengers, many of them emigrants, traveled in simpler cabins below. Its hull was divided into watertight compartments, leading many to call it ‘practically unsinkable’."],["The sinking","At 11:40 pm on 14 April 1912, lookouts spotted an iceberg directly ahead. The ship turned but scraped along it, opening its hull below the waterline. Too many compartments flooded, and at 2:20 am the Titanic broke apart and sank. Many lifeboats were launched half empty in the confusion, and most people in the freezing water died within minutes."],["Rescue","The ship’s radio operators sent distress signals, including the new SOS code. The liner Carpathia, about 93 kilometers away, raced through the ice and arrived around 4 am, rescuing about 705 survivors from the lifeboats. A closer ship, the Californian, did not respond in time."],["Changes in safety","Investigations in the United States and Britain led to the first International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea in 1914, requiring enough lifeboats for everyone on board, lifeboat drills and round-the-clock radio watches. The International Ice Patrol was set up to track icebergs in the North Atlantic."]],
k:[["Launched","1911, in Belfast"],["Length","About 269 m"],["Sank","15 April 1912"],["Deaths","More than 1,500"],["Wreck found","1985, at about 3,800 m deep"]],
d:[["1909","Construction begins in Belfast"],["31 May 1911","The Titanic is launched"],["10 Apr 1912","Sets sail from Southampton"],["14 Apr 1912","Strikes an iceberg at 11:40 pm"],["15 Apr 1912","Sinks at 2:20 am"],["1985","The wreck is found"]],
s2:[["Finding the wreck","For decades, people searched for the Titanic. In 1985, a French-American expedition led by Jean-Louis Michel and Robert Ballard found it using a deep-sea camera sled. The ship lies in two main pieces about 600 meters apart. Bacteria are slowly eating away its iron, and parts of it are collapsing."],["Women and children first","Officers generally followed the rule of ‘women and children first’ when loading lifeboats, so a much higher share of women and children survived than men. Class also mattered: first-class passengers survived at a far higher rate than those in third class, many of whom found it hard to reach the decks."]],
sp:["The band played on","The ship’s eight musicians famously continued playing to calm passengers as the Titanic sank. None of them survived. Their bravery became one of the most remembered stories of the disaster."],
g:[["Maiden voyage","A ship’s first journey"],["Hull","The main body of a ship"],["Iceberg","A large floating mass of ice broken from a glacier"],["SOS","An international distress signal in Morse code"]],
f:["The Titanic had lifeboats for only about half the people on board.","It sank about 2 hours and 40 minutes after hitting the iceberg.","The wreck lies about 3,800 meters below the surface."],
fun:"The iceberg that sank the Titanic probably broke off a glacier in Greenland.",
tl:"Ang Titanic ang pinakamalaking barko sa mundo nang maglayag ito noong 1912. Tumama ito sa isang iceberg sa unang biyahe at lumubog, na ikinamatay ng mahigit 1,500 tao, kaya nagkaroon ng mga bagong patakaran sa kaligtasan sa dagat."});

ADDART({id:"museums",t:"Museums",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🏛️",w:"Museum",r:["archaeology","sculpture","modernart","libraries","tourism"],
p:[["Keeping treasures","Museums collect, care for and display objects of art, history and science."],["Open to all","The first public museums opened in the 1600s and 1700s."],["More than displays","Museums also do research, teach and help communities remember their past."]],
l:"A museum is an institution that collects, preserves, studies and displays objects of artistic, cultural, historical or scientific importance for the public. There are art museums, history museums, natural history and science museums, open-air museums, children’s museums and many specialist museums, from toys to transport. The word comes from the Greek mouseion, a place dedicated to the Muses. Collections were once private, belonging to rulers and wealthy people, but from the 17th and 18th centuries, public museums opened their doors to everyone. Today there are around 100,000 museums worldwide. Museums also face debates about how some objects were acquired, especially during colonial times, and whether they should be returned.",
s:[["Early collections","The Mouseion of Alexandria, founded in the 3rd century BC, was a center of learning rather than a museum in the modern sense. In Renaissance Europe, wealthy collectors kept ‘cabinets of curiosities’ filled with shells, fossils, art and strange objects. Many later became the foundations of public museums."],["The first public museums","The Capitoline Museums in Rome trace their origins to 1471, when the pope gave bronze statues to the people of Rome. The Ashmolean Museum in Oxford opened in 1683. The British Museum, founded in 1753, was the first national public museum. The Louvre in Paris opened to the public in 1793, during the French Revolution."],["What museums do","Behind the galleries, curators research objects, conservators repair and protect them from light, damp and pests, and educators run programs for schools. Only a small share of most museums’ collections is on display at any time; the rest is stored and studied. Many museums now share their collections online."],["Debates","Many museums in Europe and North America hold objects taken during colonial rule, wars or excavations without consent. Countries and communities, from Nigeria to Greece and Easter Island, have asked for items to be returned. Some museums have begun returning objects, such as some of the Benin Bronzes."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek mouseion, ‘place of the Muses’"],["Oldest public collection","Capitoline Museums, Rome, 1471"],["First national museum","British Museum, 1753"],["Museums worldwide","About 100,000"],["Museum Day","18 May"]],
d:[["c.280 BC","The Mouseion of Alexandria is founded"],["1471","Origins of the Capitoline Museums in Rome"],["1683","The Ashmolean Museum opens in Oxford"],["1753","The British Museum is founded"],["1793","The Louvre opens to the public"],["1946","The International Council of Museums is founded"]],
s2:[["The Louvre","The Louvre in Paris, a former royal palace, holds about 35,000 works on display, including the Mona Lisa and the Venus de Milo, and is often the world’s most visited museum. A glass pyramid designed by architect I. M. Pei became its entrance in 1989."],["Museums without walls","Open-air museums, such as Skansen in Stockholm, founded in 1891, display historic buildings moved from across a country, with costumed guides showing traditional crafts and ways of life. Ecomuseums go further, treating a whole landscape and community as a living museum."]],
sp:["The Smithsonian","The Smithsonian Institution in the United States is the world’s largest museum, education and research complex, with 21 museums and a national zoo, most of them in Washington, D.C. It was founded in 1846 with money left by James Smithson, a British scientist who never visited the United States, and entry to its museums is free."],
g:[["Curator","A person who looks after and researches a museum collection"],["Conservator","An expert who repairs and protects objects"],["Exhibit","An object or group of objects on display"],["Repatriation","Returning objects to their place of origin"]],
f:["Only a small part of most museums’ collections is on display at any time.","International Museum Day is held on 18 May.","The Smithsonian has 21 museums."],
fun:"The Louvre’s Mona Lisa is only about 77 centimeters tall.",
tl:"Ang museo ay nangongolekta, nag-iingat at nagpapakita ng mga bagay na may kinalaman sa sining, kasaysayan at agham. Nagbukas sa publiko ang mga unang museo noong 1600 at 1700, at nagsasaliksik at nagtuturo rin ang mga ito."});

ADDART({id:"libraries",t:"Libraries",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📚",w:"Library",r:["writing","paper","print","novel","museums"],
p:[["Collections of knowledge","Libraries collect books and other materials for people to read and study."],["Ancient roots","Libraries existed in ancient Mesopotamia, Egypt and China."],["Free to all","Public libraries let anyone borrow books and use computers for free."]],
l:"A library is a collection of books and other sources of information, such as newspapers, maps, recordings and digital resources, organized so that people can find and use them. Libraries have existed for thousands of years, from clay-tablet archives in ancient Mesopotamia to the famous Library of Alexandria and the great libraries of the Islamic world, China and medieval European monasteries. Public libraries, open to everyone and free of charge, spread in the 19th century and remain important places for learning, reading and community life. Modern libraries also offer computers, internet access, digital books and help for people looking for information. Librarians organize collections and help users find what they need.",
s:[["Ancient libraries","In the 7th century BC, the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal gathered a library of thousands of clay tablets at Nineveh, including the Epic of Gilgamesh; about 30,000 tablets and fragments survive. The Library of Alexandria in Egypt, founded in the 3rd century BC, aimed to collect all the world’s knowledge. It declined over several centuries rather than in a single fire."],["Libraries of learning","In Baghdad, the House of Wisdom, flourishing in the 9th century, was a center of translation and scholarship. In Timbuktu, families kept hundreds of thousands of manuscripts on science, law and religion. In medieval Europe, monasteries copied and preserved books by hand, and the first universities built their own libraries."],["Public libraries","Printing made books cheaper, and from the 19th century, cities opened free public libraries. The Scottish-American businessman Andrew Carnegie funded more than 2,500 library buildings around the world. Systems such as the Dewey Decimal Classification, created in 1876, organized books by subject so readers could find them."],["Libraries today","Today’s libraries lend e-books and audiobooks, offer free internet, teach digital skills and host clubs and events. The world’s largest library, the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C., holds more than 170 million items. National libraries keep a copy of every book published in their country."]],
k:[["Ancient library","Ashurbanipal’s, Nineveh"],["Most famous ancient","Library of Alexandria"],["Largest today","Library of Congress"],["Book system","Dewey Decimal, 1876"],["Carnegie libraries","Over 2,500"]],
d:[["c.650 BC","Ashurbanipal’s library at Nineveh"],["c.280 BC","The Library of Alexandria is founded"],["800s","The House of Wisdom in Baghdad"],["1602","The Bodleian Library opens in Oxford"],["1800","The Library of Congress is founded"],["1876","The Dewey Decimal system is created"]],
s2:[["Saving Timbuktu’s manuscripts","In 2012, armed groups occupied Timbuktu in Mali. Librarians and families secretly smuggled hundreds of thousands of fragile manuscripts out of the city in metal trunks, by car and boat, to keep them safe. Many date from the 13th to 19th centuries."],["Libraries on the move","Where people live far from libraries, books travel to them: in buses, boats, on camels in Kenya and Mongolia, and on donkeys in Colombia, where a teacher’s ‘Biblioburro’ has carried books to rural children for decades."]],
sp:["The Dewey Decimal system","Melvil Dewey, an American librarian, created his system in 1876, dividing knowledge into ten main classes numbered from 000 to 999, such as 500 for science and 900 for history and geography. Each book gets a number based on its subject, so that books on the same topic sit together on the shelves."],
g:[["Librarian","A person who manages a library and helps people find information"],["Catalog","A list of all the items in a library"],["Archive","A collection of historical records and documents"],["E-book","A book in digital form"]],
f:["Ashurbanipal’s library included the Epic of Gilgamesh.","The Library of Congress holds more than 170 million items.","Andrew Carnegie funded over 2,500 libraries."],
fun:"Some libraries lend unusual things, such as tools, musical instruments, seeds and even fishing rods.",
tl:"Ang aklatan ay koleksiyon ng mga aklat at iba pang mapagkukunan ng impormasyon. May mga aklatan na noong sinaunang panahon, at ngayon ay malayang nakapanghihiram ng aklat at nakagagamit ng computer ang sinuman sa mga pampublikong aklatan."});

ADDART({id:"boardgames",t:"Board Games",c:"How We Live",i:"🎲",w:"Board game",r:["chess","prob","egypt","videogames","mesop"],
p:[["Ancient fun","People have played board games for at least 5,000 years."],["Luck and skill","Some games depend on dice, others on pure strategy."],["A modern boom","Board games have become popular again in the 21st century."]],
l:"A board game is a game played by moving pieces on a marked board according to rules. Board games are among the oldest forms of play: the ancient Egyptians played senet more than 5,000 years ago, and the Royal Game of Ur was played in Mesopotamia around 4,500 years ago. Games such as go, chess, mancala, backgammon and pachisi developed in different parts of the world and spread along trade routes. Some games are based on luck, using dice; others are pure strategy; many mix both. In the 20th century, mass-produced games such as Monopoly and Scrabble became household names, and since the 1990s a new wave of designer games has made board gaming a fast-growing hobby.",
s:[["The oldest games","Senet, played on a board of 30 squares, was popular in ancient Egypt and placed in tombs, including Tutankhamun’s, as it became linked to the journey of the soul. The Royal Game of Ur, found in royal tombs in Mesopotamia, was a race game played with tetrahedral dice; its rules were reconstructed from a cuneiform tablet."],["Strategy games","Go, played with black and white stones on a grid, began in China over 2,500 years ago and is known for its simple rules and enormous depth. Chess developed in India around the 6th century as chaturanga and spread through Persia and the Islamic world to Europe. Mancala games, in which seeds are sown in rows of pits, are played across Africa and Asia."],["Family games","Monopoly, published in 1935, was based on The Landlord’s Game, designed by Lizzie Magie in 1904 to show the problems of land monopolies. Scrabble was invented by architect Alfred Butts in the 1930s. Games such as ludo, adapted from India’s pachisi, and snakes and ladders, from an Indian game about virtue and vice, became family favorites."],["The modern board game boom","In 1995, The Settlers of Catan from Germany helped start a new era of ‘designer’ board games, focusing on strategy, cooperation and limited luck. Board game cafés, online crowdfunding and digital versions have helped the hobby grow, and millions of people now play regularly."]],
k:[["Oldest known","Senet, Egypt, over 5,000 years ago"],["Ancient race game","Royal Game of Ur"],["Ancient strategy game","Go, China"],["Chess origin","India, about 6th century"],["Best-selling family game","Monopoly"]],
d:[["c.3100 BC","Senet is played in Egypt"],["c.2600 BC","The Royal Game of Ur in Mesopotamia"],["c.500 BC","Go is played in China"],["c.500s","Chaturanga, an early form of chess, in India"],["1935","Monopoly is published"],["2016","AlphaGo defeats a top go player"]],
s2:[["AlphaGo","Go was long considered too complex for computers to master. In 2016, AlphaGo, an artificial intelligence program created by DeepMind, defeated Lee Sedol, one of the world’s best players, four games to one. Some of its moves surprised experts and have changed how humans play."],["Snakes and ladders","Snakes and ladders comes from an ancient Indian game called moksha patam, in which ladders represented virtues and snakes vices, teaching that good deeds lead upward and bad deeds downward. British families adopted it in the 19th century."]],
sp:["Mancala","Mancala is a family of ‘count and capture’ games played with seeds, pebbles or shells in rows of pits, often dug in the ground or carved in wood. Versions such as oware in West Africa and bao in East Africa are played with great skill and speed, and the games may be thousands of years old."],
g:[["Strategy","Planning ahead to reach a goal"],["Dice","Small cubes with numbered sides used to create chance"],["Race game","A game where players try to move pieces to the finish first"],["Mancala","A family of games played by sowing seeds in pits"]],
f:["Senet boards were found in Tutankhamun’s tomb.","Go is played on a board of 19 by 19 lines.","Monopoly grew out of a game designed to criticize landlords."],
fun:"There are more possible positions in go than there are atoms in the observable universe.",
tl:"Ang board game ay larong ginagalaw ang mga piyesa sa isang board ayon sa mga patakaran. Mahigit 5,000 taon nang nilalaro ng tao ang mga ito, mula sa senet ng Ehipto hanggang chess at go, at sumikat muli ang mga ito ngayon."});

ADDART({id:"tundra",t:"The Tundra",c:"Geography",i:"🦌",w:"Tundra",r:["arctic","antarc","climchange","iceage","glaciers"],
p:[["A treeless land","The tundra is a cold, treeless region in the far north and on high mountains."],["Frozen ground","Beneath the surface lies permafrost, ground that stays frozen all year."],["Summer life","In the short summer, flowers bloom and millions of birds arrive to nest."]],
l:"The tundra is a cold biome where trees cannot grow because of low temperatures, a short growing season and frozen ground. Arctic tundra stretches across the far north of North America, Europe and Asia, around the Arctic Ocean, and alpine tundra is found on high mountains worldwide. Winters are long, dark and bitterly cold, and summers are short and cool, with the sun barely setting. Beneath a thin layer of soil that thaws each summer lies permafrost, ground that remains frozen for years. Mosses, lichens, grasses, low shrubs and flowers grow close to the ground. Animals such as caribou, musk oxen, Arctic foxes and lemmings live there, and millions of birds migrate there to breed.",
s:[["Climate","Arctic tundra winters can fall below −30°C, with weeks of near-total darkness. The growing season lasts only about 50 to 60 days. Although the land is often wet in summer, the tundra receives little precipitation, about as little as some deserts, because cold air holds little moisture. Strong winds sweep across the open land."],["Plants","Tundra plants are small and grow close to the ground to escape the wind and absorb warmth. Many have hairy stems or dark leaves to trap heat. In summer, the tundra bursts into bloom with Arctic poppies, saxifrages and cotton grass. Lichens, an important food for caribou, can survive the harshest conditions."],["Animals","Caribou and reindeer migrate across the tundra in huge herds. Musk oxen have thick, shaggy coats. Arctic foxes, Arctic hares and ptarmigans turn white in winter. Lemmings live under the snow, and their populations rise and fall in cycles, affecting predators such as snowy owls. In summer, swarms of insects feed millions of nesting birds."],["Peoples and change","Indigenous peoples such as the Inuit, Sámi, Nenets and others have lived on the tundra for thousands of years, herding reindeer, hunting and fishing. Today the Arctic is warming nearly four times faster than the global average. Thawing permafrost damages roads and buildings and releases carbon dioxide and methane, which add to climate change, while shrubs and trees are spreading north."]],
k:[["Type","A cold, treeless biome"],["Frozen ground","Permafrost"],["Growing season","About 50–60 days"],["Typical animals","Caribou, Arctic fox, lemming"],["Warming","Nearly four times the global average (Arctic)"]],
d:[["c.20,000 years ago","Tundra covers much of Ice Age Europe and Asia"],["c.4,000 years ago","The last woolly mammoths die out on Wrangel Island"],["1600s–1800s","Explorers and traders map the Arctic tundra"],["1970s","Scientists begin monitoring permafrost temperatures"],["2022","Study finds the Arctic warming nearly four times faster"]],
s2:[["The permafrost carbon store","Arctic permafrost holds roughly twice as much carbon as there is in the atmosphere today, in the remains of plants and animals frozen for thousands of years. As it thaws, microbes break this matter down, releasing greenhouse gases. Scientists study how fast this may happen and how much it could add to warming."],["Reindeer herders","The Sámi of northern Scandinavia and the Nenets of Arctic Russia follow their reindeer herds on long seasonal migrations across the tundra. Reindeer provide meat, hides and transport. Warmer winters with rain that freezes into ice over lichens can make it hard for reindeer to find food."]],
sp:["The midnight sun","North of the Arctic Circle, the sun does not set for days or weeks around the summer solstice, a phenomenon called the midnight sun. Plants grow around the clock during this time, making the most of the short summer, while in winter the sun may not rise at all."],
g:[["Biome","A large area with a particular climate, plants and animals"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen for two or more years"],["Lichen","A partnership of a fungus and an alga"],["Alpine","Relating to high mountains"]],
f:["The tundra receives about as little precipitation as some deserts.","The word tundra comes from a Sámi word meaning ‘treeless plain’ or ‘high land’.","Arctic foxes can survive temperatures of around −50°C."],
fun:"Lemmings do not jump off cliffs; that myth was spread partly by a staged scene in a 1958 nature film.",
tl:"Ang tundra ay malamig na lupaing walang puno sa dulong hilaga at sa matataas na bundok. Sa ilalim nito ay may permafrost na laging nagyeyelo, at sa maikling tag-init ay namumulaklak ang mga halaman at dumarating ang milyun-milyong ibon."});

ADDART({id:"mammoth",t:"Woolly Mammoths",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦣",w:"Woolly mammoth",r:["elephant","iceage","extinction","fossils","caveart"],
p:[["Ice Age giants","Woolly mammoths were huge, hairy relatives of elephants that lived during the Ice Age."],["Built for cold","They had thick fur, small ears and a layer of fat to survive freezing steppes."],["Gone but not forgotten","The last mammoths died out about 4,000 years ago."]],
l:"The woolly mammoth was a species of elephant that lived on the cold, grassy steppes of northern Europe, Asia and North America during the last Ice Age. About the size of a modern African elephant, standing around 3 meters tall at the shoulder, it was covered in a thick coat of long hair over a woolly underfur, with small ears, a short tail and a layer of fat to keep it warm. Its long, curved tusks could reach more than 4 meters. Most woolly mammoths died out around 10,000 years ago as the climate warmed and their habitat shrank, and as humans hunted them, but a small population survived on Wrangel Island in the Arctic until about 4,000 years ago.",
s:[["Life on the steppe","Woolly mammoths grazed on grasses, herbs and shrubs on the ‘mammoth steppe’, a vast cold grassland that stretched from Spain to Canada during the Ice Age. Like elephants today, they probably lived in family herds led by older females. They used their tusks to clear snow and to fight, and their ridged teeth to grind tough plants."],["Mammoths and people","Early humans hunted mammoths for meat, hides and bones. In Ukraine and Russia, people built shelters from hundreds of mammoth bones. Mammoths appear in cave paintings and carvings, such as those in Rouffignac Cave in France, showing that people knew them well."],["Frozen in time","Because mammoths lived in places with permafrost, some carcasses have been found frozen, with skin, hair and even stomach contents preserved. In 2007, a reindeer herder in Siberia found Lyuba, a month-old baby mammoth about 42,000 years old, one of the best-preserved ever discovered."],["Why they disappeared","Scientists think a combination of a warming climate, which turned grasslands into forests and wetlands, and human hunting drove mammoths to extinction. The Wrangel Island mammoths survived for thousands of years after the pyramids of Egypt were first built. Some companies are now trying to use genetic engineering to create cold-adapted elephants with mammoth traits."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Mammuthus primigenius"],["Height","About 3 m at the shoulder"],["Tusks","Up to over 4 m"],["Closest living relative","Asian elephant"],["Last survivors","Wrangel Island, about 4,000 years ago"]],
d:[["c.400,000 years ago","Woolly mammoths appear"],["c.40,000–10,000 years ago","Humans hunt mammoths and paint them in caves"],["c.10,000 years ago","Most mammoths die out"],["c.4,000 years ago","The last mammoths die on Wrangel Island"],["1799","A frozen mammoth is found in Siberia"],["2007","Baby mammoth Lyuba is discovered"]],
s2:[["Lyuba","Lyuba was found in 2007 by a reindeer herder on the Yamal Peninsula in Siberia. The baby mammoth was so well preserved that scientists could see her trunk, eyes and hair, and found her mother’s milk in her stomach. They think she drowned in mud, which preserved her body."],["Mammoth bone houses","At sites such as Mezhyrich in Ukraine, Ice Age people built round huts using the bones and tusks of dozens of mammoths, probably covered with hides. Some structures used bones from more than 60 animals, showing how important mammoths were to human survival."]],
sp:["Mammoths and elephants","Woolly mammoths were most closely related to Asian elephants, from which their lineage split millions of years ago. DNA from frozen mammoths has allowed scientists to read much of their genome and discover genes for thick hair, fat storage and tolerance of cold."],
g:[["Steppe","A large, dry, treeless grassland"],["Tusk","A very long tooth that sticks out of an animal’s mouth"],["Extinction","When a species dies out completely"],["Genome","All the genetic information of an organism"]],
f:["Woolly mammoths were about the same size as African elephants.","Some mammoths survived until about 4,000 years ago.","Ivory from mammoth tusks is still found in the Siberian permafrost."],
fun:"Some woolly mammoths were still alive when the Great Pyramid of Giza was built.",
tl:"Ang woolly mammoth ay malaking mabalahibong kamag-anak ng elepante na nabuhay noong Panahon ng Yelo. May makapal itong balahibo at maliliit na tainga para sa lamig, at namatay ang huling mammoth mga 4,000 taon na ang nakararaan."});

ADDART({id:"neanderthals",t:"Neanderthals",c:"History",i:"🦴",w:"Neanderthal",r:["humanorigins","stoneage","dna","caveart","iceage"],
p:[["Our close relatives","Neanderthals were ancient humans who lived in Europe and western Asia."],["Skilled and clever","They made tools, controlled fire, buried their dead and may have made art."],["Part of us","Most people today carry a little Neanderthal DNA."]],
l:"Neanderthals were an extinct species of human, Homo neanderthalensis, who lived across Europe and western and central Asia from about 400,000 to about 40,000 years ago. They were our closest known relatives, sharing a common ancestor with modern humans that lived several hundred thousand years ago. Neanderthals were stocky and strong, adapted to cold climates, with brains as large as or larger than ours. They made sophisticated stone tools, hunted large animals, controlled fire, cared for the sick and buried their dead. When modern humans spread out of Africa, the two groups met and had children together, and today people of non-African ancestry carry about 1 to 2 percent Neanderthal DNA.",
s:[["Discovery","The first Neanderthal fossil to be recognized as a different kind of human was found in 1856 in the Neander Valley near Düsseldorf, Germany, which gave the species its name. Earlier finds in Belgium and Gibraltar were only later identified as Neanderthals. Hundreds of fossils have since been found from Spain to Siberia."],["Life and skills","Neanderthals made stone tools using a careful technique to prepare and strike flakes. They hunted mammoths, bison and deer, and also ate plants and seafood. They made fire, used birch tar as glue, and wore clothing made from hides. Some fossils show people who survived serious injuries, suggesting they were cared for by others."],["Minds and culture","Neanderthals buried their dead, and some sites have evidence of pigments, shells and eagle talons used as ornaments. Cave paintings in Spain have been dated to over 64,000 years ago, before modern humans arrived, suggesting Neanderthals may have made art, though this is debated. Whether they could speak as we do is unknown."],["Disappearance","Neanderthals vanished around 40,000 years ago, not long after modern humans reached Europe. Possible reasons include competition with modern humans, small and isolated populations, climate swings and interbreeding that absorbed them into modern populations. In a sense, they did not disappear entirely: their genes live on in us."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Homo neanderthalensis"],["Lived","About 400,000–40,000 years ago"],["Range","Europe and western and central Asia"],["First recognized","1856, Neander Valley, Germany"],["DNA in people today","About 1–2% (non-Africans)"]],
d:[["c.400,000 years ago","Neanderthals emerge in Europe"],["c.64,000 years ago","Possible Neanderthal cave paintings in Spain"],["c.45,000 years ago","Modern humans spread into Europe"],["c.40,000 years ago","Neanderthals disappear"],["1856","Fossil found in the Neander Valley"],["2010","The Neanderthal genome is published"]],
s2:[["Reading ancient DNA","Swedish scientist Svante Pääbo and his team extracted and sequenced DNA from Neanderthal bones, publishing a draft genome in 2010. It showed that Neanderthals and modern humans had interbred. He also discovered the Denisovans, another ancient human group, from a finger bone found in Siberia. He won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2022."],["Neanderthal genes today","Some Neanderthal genes still affect people today, influencing skin, hair, the immune system and the risk of some diseases. Scientists think certain genes may have helped modern humans adapt to new environments when they spread out of Africa."]],
sp:["Not so brutish","Early reconstructions showed Neanderthals as hunched, brutish cave men, partly based on the skeleton of an old man with arthritis. Modern research shows they walked upright like us, were skilled toolmakers and lived in complex social groups. Dressed in modern clothes, a Neanderthal might not stand out much in a crowd."],
g:[["Species","A group of living things that can breed together"],["Fossil","The preserved remains of an ancient living thing"],["Genome","All the genetic information of an organism"],["Interbreeding","Different groups having children together"]],
f:["Neanderthals had brains as large as, or larger than, modern humans.","Most people outside Africa have some Neanderthal DNA.","The species was named after the Neander Valley in Germany."],
fun:"Neanderthals used birch tar as glue to attach stone tools to wooden handles.",
tl:"Ang mga Neanderthal ay sinaunang tao na nanirahan sa Europa at kanlurang Asya. Gumawa sila ng kasangkapan, gumamit ng apoy at naglibing ng patay, at karamihan sa mga tao ngayon ay may kaunting DNA nila."});

ADDART({id:"suezcanal",t:"The Suez Canal",c:"Geography",i:"⚓",w:"Suez Canal",r:["egyptcountry","ships","trade","coldwar","panamacountry"],
p:[["A shortcut between seas","The Suez Canal links the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea through Egypt."],["Saving thousands of kilometers","Ships can avoid sailing around Africa."],["A vital trade route","A large share of world trade passes through it."]],
l:"The Suez Canal is an artificial waterway in Egypt, about 193 kilometers long, connecting the Mediterranean Sea at Port Said with the Red Sea at Suez. Opened in 1869, it allows ships to travel between Europe and Asia without sailing around the southern tip of Africa, cutting thousands of kilometers from the journey. It has no locks, because the two seas are at nearly the same level. The canal is one of the most important shipping routes in the world, normally carrying around a tenth of global trade, including oil, container ships and grain. Its control has been the subject of conflict, most famously in the Suez Crisis of 1956, and it remains a key source of income for Egypt.",
s:[["Building the canal","Ancient Egyptian rulers dug canals linking the Nile to the Red Sea, but they silted up. The French diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps organized the Suez Canal Company, which began work in 1859. Tens of thousands of Egyptian laborers, many forced to work, dug the canal, first largely by hand and later with dredging machines. Thousands died. It opened on 17 November 1869."],["The Suez Crisis","Britain bought Egypt’s shares in the canal company in 1875, and British troops controlled the canal zone for decades. In 1956, Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalized the canal. Britain, France and Israel invaded, but pressure from the United States, the Soviet Union and the United Nations forced them to withdraw, a major moment in the end of the colonial era."],["Closures","After the Six-Day War of 1967, the canal was closed for eight years, trapping 15 cargo ships, known as the ‘Yellow Fleet’, in its lakes until 1975. In 2015, Egypt opened a new parallel channel to allow two-way traffic along part of the route."],["A global chokepoint","Because so much trade passes through it, any disruption affects the world economy. In March 2021, the giant container ship Ever Given ran aground and blocked the canal for six days, holding up hundreds of ships. Since late 2023, attacks on shipping in the Red Sea have led many ships to avoid the route and sail around Africa instead."]],
k:[["Country","Egypt"],["Length","About 193 km"],["Opened","17 November 1869"],["Connects","Mediterranean and Red Seas"],["Locks","None"]],
d:[["1859","Construction begins"],["1869","The canal opens"],["1875","Britain buys Egypt’s shares"],["1956","Nationalized by Egypt; the Suez Crisis"],["1967–75","Closed after the Six-Day War"],["2021","The Ever Given blocks the canal for six days"]],
s2:[["The Ever Given","On 23 March 2021, strong winds pushed the Ever Given, a 400-meter-long container ship, sideways across the canal, wedging it into both banks. Tugboats and dredgers worked for six days to free it, while more than 400 ships waited at each end. The blockage held up billions of dollars’ worth of trade each day."],["The Yellow Fleet","When the canal was suddenly closed in June 1967, 15 ships from eight countries were stranded in the Great Bitter Lake. Their crews formed a community, holding their own ‘Olympic Games’ and issuing stamps. The ships became covered in desert sand, giving them their name. Only two could sail away under their own power when the canal reopened in 1975."]],
sp:["The Panama Canal","The Suez Canal’s counterpart in the Americas, the Panama Canal, opened in 1914 and links the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. Unlike Suez, it uses locks to lift ships about 26 meters over the hills of Panama to a large artificial lake and back down again."],
g:[["Canal","An artificial waterway for boats or irrigation"],["Lock","A section of canal with gates that raises or lowers boats"],["Chokepoint","A narrow route where traffic can easily be blocked"],["Nationalize","When a government takes over ownership of a company"]],
f:["The Suez Canal is about 193 kilometers long.","It has no locks.","The Ever Given blocked it for six days in 2021."],
fun:"Going through the Suez Canal can save a ship sailing between Europe and Asia several thousand kilometers and more than a week at sea.",
tl:"Ang Suez Canal ay daluyang-tubig sa Ehipto na nag-uugnay sa Dagat Mediteraneo at Dagat Pula. Hindi na kailangang umikot ang mga barko sa Africa, kaya mahalaga itong ruta ng pandaigdigang kalakalan."});

ADDART({id:"concrete",t:"Concrete",c:"Technology",i:"🏗️",w:"Concrete",r:["skyscrapers","bridges","rome","climchange","architecture"],
p:[["The world’s building material","Concrete is the most widely used human-made material on Earth."],["Ancient invention","The Romans built with concrete 2,000 years ago."],["A climate challenge","Making cement produces a large share of the world’s carbon emissions."]],
l:"Concrete is a building material made by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel. When mixed, it can be poured into almost any shape, and it hardens into an artificial stone through a chemical reaction called hydration. Concrete is the most widely used human-made material in the world; by some estimates, only water is used in greater quantities. It forms the foundations, floors, walls, roads, bridges, dams and towers of the modern world. The Romans used a form of concrete to build harbors, aqueducts and the dome of the Pantheon. Modern concrete, based on Portland cement and often strengthened with steel bars, spread in the 19th and 20th centuries. Making cement releases large amounts of carbon dioxide, so researchers are developing greener alternatives.",
s:[["How concrete works","Cement is a fine powder made by heating limestone and clay to about 1,450°C in a kiln. When mixed with water, it reacts to form crystals that bind the sand and gravel together. Concrete keeps getting harder for weeks, months and even years. It is very strong when squeezed but weak when pulled or bent, so it is often reinforced with steel."],["Roman concrete","The Romans mixed lime with volcanic ash from near Naples, called pozzolana, to make a concrete that could set underwater. Roman harbor walls have survived the sea for 2,000 years. The Pantheon in Rome, completed around AD 125, still has the world’s largest unreinforced concrete dome. Scientists have found that Roman concrete can heal its own small cracks."],["Modern concrete","In 1824, the English bricklayer Joseph Aspdin patented Portland cement, named after the color of stone from the Isle of Portland. In the mid-1800s, French inventors began adding iron and steel to concrete, creating reinforced concrete, which made modern skyscrapers, long bridges and huge dams possible."],["Concrete and the climate","Cement production is responsible for about 8 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, partly from burning fuel to heat kilns and partly from the chemical change in limestone. Researchers are developing cements that use less energy, replacing part of the cement with materials such as fly ash or clay, and capturing carbon dioxide during production."]],
k:[["Ingredients","Cement, water, sand, gravel"],["Hardens by","Hydration"],["Modern cement","Portland cement, 1824"],["Famous Roman example","The Pantheon dome"],["Share of CO₂ emissions","About 8% (cement)"]],
d:[["c.200 BC","Romans widely use concrete"],["c.AD 125","The Pantheon’s dome is completed"],["1824","Portland cement is patented"],["1867","Joseph Monier patents reinforced concrete flowerpots"],["1936","The Hoover Dam is completed"],["2010s–","Low-carbon cements are developed"]],
s2:[["The Pantheon","The Pantheon in Rome has a concrete dome 43.3 meters across, with a circular opening called the oculus at the top. Its builders used heavier materials at the base of the dome and lighter volcanic stone near the top to reduce its weight. Nearly 1,900 years later, it is still used as a church."],["The Hoover Dam","The Hoover Dam on the Colorado River in the United States, completed in 1936, used about 2.5 million cubic meters of concrete. Because concrete gives off heat as it sets, engineers poured it in blocks and ran cold water through pipes inside to cool it; otherwise it would have taken over a century to cool."]],
sp:["Reinforced concrete","Steel bars, called rebar, are placed inside concrete before it sets. The concrete resists squeezing, and the steel resists stretching, so together they make beams and columns far stronger than either alone. A French gardener, Joseph Monier, patented the idea in 1867 to make stronger flowerpots."],
g:[["Cement","A powder that binds concrete together when mixed with water"],["Aggregate","The sand and gravel in concrete"],["Hydration","The chemical reaction between cement and water"],["Rebar","Steel bars used to reinforce concrete"]],
f:["Concrete is the most widely used human-made material.","The Pantheon’s dome has lasted nearly 1,900 years.","Concrete keeps getting stronger for years after it is poured."],
fun:"Reinforced concrete was patented by a gardener who wanted stronger flowerpots.",
tl:"Ang kongkreto ay materyales sa pagtatayo na gawa sa semento, tubig, buhangin at graba. Ito ang pinakamalawak na ginagamit na gawang-taong materyales, ginamit na ng mga Romano, ngunit malaki ang ambag ng paggawa ng semento sa carbon emissions."});

ADDART({id:"carthage",t:"Carthage",c:"History",i:"🐘",w:"Ancient Carthage",r:["phoenicians","rome","tunisiacountry","ships","greece"],
p:[["A Phoenician city","Carthage was founded by Phoenicians in North Africa, in what is now Tunisia."],["A sea empire","It became a great trading and naval power in the western Mediterranean."],["Rome’s rival","It fought three wars with Rome and was destroyed in 146 BC."]],
l:"Carthage was an ancient city on the coast of North Africa, near present-day Tunis in Tunisia, founded by Phoenician settlers from the city of Tyre, traditionally in 814 BC. It grew into the capital of a powerful maritime empire that controlled trade across the western Mediterranean, with territories in North Africa, Spain, Sardinia and Sicily. Carthage clashed with the Greeks and then with the rising power of Rome in three Punic Wars. Its general Hannibal famously crossed the Alps with elephants to invade Italy. Rome finally destroyed Carthage in 146 BC, but later rebuilt it as a Roman city, which became one of the largest in the empire. Its ruins are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["Founding","According to legend, Carthage was founded by Queen Elissa, also called Dido, who fled Tyre. Offered as much land as an oxhide could cover, she cut the hide into thin strips to surround a whole hill. The name Carthage comes from Phoenician words meaning ‘new city’. Its people spoke Punic, a form of Phoenician."],["A trading empire","Carthage grew rich from trade in metals, textiles, purple dye, ivory and slaves, and from farming the fertile lands of North Africa. It was governed by a council of wealthy families and elected officials called suffetes. Its famous circular military harbor, the cothon, could shelter a large fleet of warships."],["The Punic Wars","Rome and Carthage fought three wars between 264 and 146 BC. In the First, Rome built a navy and won Sicily. In the Second, Hannibal led an army with war elephants from Spain over the Alps into Italy in 218 BC and crushed the Romans at Cannae in 216 BC, but he was eventually defeated at Zama in 202 BC. In the Third, Rome besieged and destroyed Carthage."],["Roman and later Carthage","A century later, Julius Caesar and Augustus refounded Carthage as a Roman colony, and it became the capital of the province of Africa and a center of early Christianity. It was captured by the Vandals in 439, retaken by the Byzantine Empire and conquered by Arab armies in 698. Nearby Tunis later grew into the region’s main city."]],
k:[["Location","Near Tunis, Tunisia"],["Founded","Traditionally 814 BC, by Phoenicians"],["Famous general","Hannibal"],["Destroyed by Rome","146 BC"],["UNESCO site","Since 1979"]],
d:[["814 BC","Traditional founding of Carthage"],["264–241 BC","First Punic War"],["218 BC","Hannibal crosses the Alps"],["216 BC","Hannibal wins the Battle of Cannae"],["146 BC","Rome destroys Carthage"],["698","Conquered by Arab armies"]],
s2:[["Hannibal’s elephants","In 218 BC, Hannibal led an army of tens of thousands of soldiers, horses and around 37 war elephants from Spain across the Pyrenees and the Alps into Italy. Many men and nearly all the elephants died from cold and hardship, but the crossing stunned Rome. Historians still debate which pass he used."],["‘Carthage must be destroyed’","The Roman senator Cato the Elder was said to end every speech, whatever the subject, with the words ‘Carthage must be destroyed’. Rome’s fear and hatred of its rival led to the Third Punic War and the city’s destruction in 146 BC."]],
sp:["The cothon","Carthage’s military harbor was a circular basin with an island in the middle, where the admiral’s headquarters stood. Covered sheds around the edge could hold over 200 warships. The outline of the harbor can still be seen today in a suburb of Tunis."],
g:[["Punic","Relating to Carthage and its people"],["Phoenicians","A seafaring people of the eastern Mediterranean"],["Suffete","An elected chief official of Carthage"],["Siege","Surrounding a city to force it to surrender"]],
f:["Carthage’s name means ‘new city’.","Hannibal crossed the Alps with war elephants.","Rome rebuilt Carthage about a century after destroying it."],
fun:"A Roman story claims the Romans sowed salt on Carthage’s fields, but historians think this was invented much later.",
tl:"Ang Carthage ay sinaunang lungsod sa Hilagang Africa, sa ngayon ay Tunisia, na itinatag ng mga Phoenician. Naging makapangyarihang imperyo ito sa dagat at kalaban ng Roma, hanggang wasakin ito noong 146 BC."});

ADDART({id:"asante",t:"The Asante Empire",c:"History",i:"👑",w:"Ashanti Empire",r:["ghanacountry","africacolon","slavetrade","mali","benin"],
p:[["A West African power","The Asante Empire was a powerful kingdom in what is now Ghana."],["The Golden Stool","A sacred stool covered in gold symbolized the soul of the nation."],["Resisting Britain","The Asante fought several wars against British forces in the 1800s."]],
l:"The Asante Empire, also spelled Ashanti, was a powerful state of the Akan people in what is now Ghana, in West Africa. Founded around 1701 by Osei Tutu, it grew through conquest and trade to dominate much of the region during the 18th and 19th centuries, from its capital at Kumasi. The Asante grew rich from gold, kola nuts and trade with Europeans on the coast, including, for a long period, the trade in enslaved people. Their kingdom had a sophisticated government, roads and a strong army. The Asante fought a series of wars against the British between 1824 and 1900 before their territory was made a British colony. The Asantehene, the Asante king, remains an influential traditional ruler today.",
s:[["Founding and the Golden Stool","According to tradition, around 1701 the priest Okomfo Anokye called down from the sky a stool covered in gold, which landed on the knees of Osei Tutu, making him the first Asantehene. The Golden Stool was believed to hold the spirit of the Asante nation. It is so sacred that no one, not even the king, sits on it."],["Power and wealth","Kumasi became a large, well-planned city linked by great roads to the rest of the empire. The Asante controlled rich goldfields, and gold dust was used as currency, weighed with small brass weights shaped like animals and symbols. The state also traded kola nuts across West Africa and, until the early 19th century, sold captives to European slave traders on the coast."],["Wars with Britain","As Britain expanded from its coastal forts, conflict grew. The Asante defeated a British force in 1824, but British troops burned Kumasi in 1874. In 1896, the British exiled the Asantehene Prempeh I. When the British governor demanded the Golden Stool in 1900, the Queen Mother Yaa Asantewaa led a final war of resistance."],["Asante today","After the war, the Asante lands became part of the British Gold Coast colony, which gained independence as Ghana in 1957. The Asantehene was restored as a traditional ruler and today plays an important cultural and ceremonial role. Kente cloth, woven in bright strips of colored silk and cotton, is one of the best-known symbols of Asante culture."]],
k:[["Location","Present-day Ghana"],["Founded","About 1701"],["Founder","Osei Tutu"],["Capital","Kumasi"],["Sacred symbol","The Golden Stool"]],
d:[["c.1701","Osei Tutu founds the Asante Empire"],["1700s","The empire expands across the region"],["1824","Asante forces defeat a British army"],["1874","British troops burn Kumasi"],["1900","Yaa Asantewaa leads the War of the Golden Stool"],["1902","Asante becomes a British colony"]],
s2:[["Yaa Asantewaa","In 1900, when the British governor demanded that the Golden Stool be brought to him so he could sit on it, Yaa Asantewaa, Queen Mother of Ejisu, rallied the Asante to fight. She led a siege of the British fort at Kumasi for months before being captured and exiled to the Seychelles. She is honored in Ghana as a hero of resistance to colonial rule."],["Kente cloth","Kente is woven on narrow looms into long strips that are sewn together to make large cloths. Each pattern and color has a name and meaning, such as gold for wealth and green for growth. Once reserved for royalty, it is now worn on special occasions throughout Ghana and by people of African descent around the world."]],
sp:["Gold weights","Asante goldsmiths and traders used thousands of tiny brass weights to measure gold dust. Made by the lost-wax casting method, they were shaped like animals, people, tools and symbols, many illustrating proverbs. Today they are prized as works of art."],
g:[["Asantehene","The king of the Asante"],["Akan","A large group of peoples in Ghana and Côte d’Ivoire"],["Kente","A brightly colored woven cloth of Ghana"],["Queen Mother","A senior female leader in Akan societies"]],
f:["No one, not even the king, sits on the Golden Stool.","Gold dust was used as money in the Asante Empire.","Kente cloth is woven in narrow strips."],
fun:"The Golden Stool was hidden during the 1900 war, and the British never captured it.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Asante ay makapangyarihang kaharian sa ngayon ay Ghana sa Kanlurang Africa. Sinasagisag ng Golden Stool ang kaluluwa ng bansa, at ilang ulit silang lumaban sa mga British noong 1800."});
